# Getting Started, part 2: installing.  A test compares the options with the
# help of the install script.
.h1 Installing Strata [installing]
Strata is installed with the install script.  The script puts the programs, the
help and the manuals under one directory, which is called the
{b:prefix directory}, and installs the programs that Strata needs to run.
Strata runs on Linux, and has been tested on Debian and Ubuntu.
.h2 In short [install-short]
The install script is delivered with the programs and installs them.  Run it
from the directory that holds it.
.h3 As an administrator [install-admin]
The default prefix directory is {c:/usr}, which only an administrator can write:
.code
sudo scripts/strata-install
.end
.h3 In your home directory [install-home]
Without administrator privileges the script installs into the directory {c:usr}
in your home directory.  Nothing outside the prefix directory is written:
.code
scripts/strata-install
.end
The programs of the list in {x:prereqs} are optional for running Strata, so a
missing one never stops the install: Strata is installed, and a warning at the
end of the output lists each missing program, what is lost without it, and the
{c:apt-get} command that an administrator runs.  Nothing about the missing
programs is printed earlier, where it could scroll off the screen.  The option
{c:--no-prereqs} installs Strata without looking for the programs and prints no
warning.  To install into another directory that you own, give {c:--prefix};
the same rules apply.
.h3 Starting Strata after the install [install-start]
Start the Document Manager.  After an install under {c:/usr} that is one
command:
.code
/usr/bin/str
.end
The window version has {c:-x11} at the end of the name:
.code
/usr/bin/str-x11
.end
After an install in your home directory, give the directory of that prefix
directory in front of the name:
.code
${{HOME}}/usr/bin/str
.end
.h2 The options of the install script [install-options]
.table 24 52
Option | What the option does
{c:--prefix DIR} | Installs under the prefix directory DIR.  The default is
 {c:/usr} for an administrator and {c:${{HOME}}/usr} for anybody else.
{c:--destdir DIR} | Puts everything under DIR as well, to make a package.
{c:--check} | Checks which of the programs that Strata requires are on the
 system; nothing is installed.
{c:--dry-run} | Reports what would be done; nothing is done.
{c:--uninstall} | Removes what an install under the same prefix directory put
 there.
{c:--no-prereqs} | Installs Strata alone: the programs that Strata can use are
 neither looked for nor installed, and no warning about them is printed.
{c:--with-prereqs} | The usual behaviour: the programs that Strata can use are
 looked for.  An administrator is asked, and the missing ones are installed; for
 anybody else the install is made, and a warning at the end lists the missing
 ones.
{c:--yes} | Unattended: nothing is asked, and what was listed is installed.
{c:--list-prereqs} | Shows which of the programs that Strata requires are
 present and which are missing, for an administrator with the sizes of the
 packages; nothing is installed.
{c:--quiet} | Prints only warnings and errors.
.end
.h2 What is installed [installed]
The prefix directory gets this layout.  The paths are relative to the prefix
directory:
.table 32 42
Directory | What the directory is for
{c:bin} | The programs: the Document Manager, the Editor, the Printer and the
 Viewer, and the window versions of the Document Manager and the Editor.
{c:share/strata} | The help file, and the manifest, which lists what the
 install made.
{c:share/strata/doc} | The documents of Strata, in the format of Strata: open
 them in the Editor.  They are the Reference Manual, Getting Started, the key
 cards and the Errata.
{c:share/strata/pdf} | The manuals as PDF files, to print or to read.
{c:share/strata/tutorial} | The documents that the Tutorial teaches with.
{c:share/strata/templates} | The templates of the New command.
{c:share/doc/strata} | The licence, the README and the known issues.
.end
At the end of an install a summary shows where each group of files went.
.h3 Removing Strata [uninstalling]
.code
scripts/strata-install --uninstall --prefix ${{HOME}}/usr
.end
removes exactly the files that the manifest lists, and the directories that the
install made once they are empty.  Files of your own in the prefix directory
stay.  Packages that an administrator's install put in with {c:apt} stay,
because other programs may use them.
.h2 The programs that Strata needs [prereqs]
All of these programs are optional for running Strata, and the table in
{x:software} says what each one is for.  Printing and Preview need LaTeX, the
equations in the window version need {c:dvipng}, the pictures in a print need
ghostscript, and the spell check and the thesaurus need a spelling program and
the thesaurus data.  The first step of the install script is a check of the
system, which lists which of them are present and which are missing:
.code
What Strata needs to run:
  present  LaTeX: Print to PDF, Preview and the equations on the screen
  missing  dvipng: equations, keycaps and pictures in the window version
  present  ghostscript: pictures in a print
  present  spell check: aspell or hunspell
  missing  thesaurus: Tools, Thesaurus
.end
What happens next depends on who runs the script.
.h3 As an administrator [prereqs-admin]
If something is missing, a table shows the packages that would be installed:
the name, the version, the estimated download size and installed size, and what
the package is needed for, with the totals.  The sizes are estimates from the
index of packages of the system.  Nothing is installed before the table has
been shown.  Then this question is shown:
.code
Install these packages? [y/N]
.end
Only {k:y} goes on.  Any other answer, or just {k:Enter}, stops the script
before anything is installed, and the prefix directory is left as it was; to
install Strata alone, run the script again with {c:--no-prereqs}.  The option
{c:--yes} makes the install unattended: nothing is asked, and what was listed is
installed.  A script without a terminal must have {c:--yes}, or nothing is
installed.  The packages of all the programs are up to about 700 MB on a system
that has no TeX Live; your system may need fewer.
The packages are installed with {c:apt}, into the system, and they are there
for every user.  On a fresh system the package index may be old, so that the
mirrors no longer have some of the versions that the index names and the
install fails with {c:404 Not Found}.  The script then offers to run
{c:apt-get update} and to try again once; with {c:--yes} that is done without
asking.  A failure of another kind, such as no free disk space, is reported as
it is.
.h3 Without administrator privileges [prereqs-user]
Every one of these programs is optional, so a missing one never stops the
install.  Strata is installed, the exit status is 0, and the last block of the
output is a warning that begins with this line:
.code
Optional programs are missing:
.end
It lists each missing package with what is lost without it, and ends with the
command that an administrator runs, for example:
.code
sudo apt-get install --no-install-recommends dvipng mythes-en-us
.end
When an administrator has installed them, Strata uses them at once; the install
need not be run again.  The programs can also be installed by running the
script as an administrator, after a question.  What only an administrator can
set up is not installed: the print queues, the libraries of the window version
and a viewer for PDF files.  A system that has no {c:apt} gets the same list,
with the advice to install the packages with its own package manager.  The
option {c:--no-prereqs} installs Strata without looking for the programs and
prints no warning; Strata then starts, and a box names what each missing
program is for.
